    "title": "UNDERWATER COMMUNICATION SYSTEM FOR SCUBA DIVERS",
    "authors": [
      "Snehal Borhade",
      "Janvi Mahapadi",
      "Samrudhi Bandi",
      "Dr. S.B. Dhonde"
    ],
    "abstract": "Underwater wireless communication (UWC) plays a critical role in applications such as tactical surveillance, marine biology monitoring, pollution detection, and underwater infrastructure maintenance. Traditional radio frequency (RF) communication methods, including Wi-Fi, are ineffective underwater due to rapid absorption of radio waves. In contrast, optical wireless communication (OWC) using light, particularly Li-Fi offers a promising alternative, as light can penetrate water more effectively. This paper presents a laser -based underwater communication system designed for real -time audio transmission. Pulse Amplitude Modulation (PAM) is employed to encode audio signals into laser pulses, which are transmitted through water and received by a solar panel functioning as a photodetector. A comprehensive literature review highlights the use of different laser wavelengths to optimize transmission efficiency, range, and error rates, and discusses acknowledgment mechanisms to detect signal loss. Experimental results demonstrate successful audio transmission in water over a 1 -meter distance and text transmission in air over 5 meters using a line -of-sight setup, with minimal laten cy. Key challenges encountered include beam divergence, water turbidity, and alignment sensitivity. Despite range limitations typically reported in literature (7 –10 meters underwater), our prototype illustrates the potential for low-cost, energy -efficient laser-based systems in applications such as diver communication, marine research, and underwater sensor networks."
  }
